    "description": "Management skills concern how organizations coordinate people, information, and resources. Documented Iranian examples include management education at the Industrial Management Institute and practical knowledge-management training involving public-sector institutions.",
    "tags": ["Management education", "Organizational learning", "Knowledge management", "Productivity", "Professional development"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:49:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:49:52Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Management Skills and Training in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand management education and practical organizational learning in Iran through documented institutions and training examples."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "management-education",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A field spanning organizational functions",
        "content": "Management skills are best understood as a collection of capabilities rather than a single qualification. Finance, staffing, marketing, and operational coordination involve different bodies of knowledge. The World Higher Education Database, maintained by the International Association of Universities, illustrates this breadth in its entry for Iran’s Industrial Management Institute, or IMI. Its listed fields include finance, human resources, industrial management, marketing, and transport management. [s1](https://whed.net/institutions/IAU-016832)\n\nThe database records IMI’s foundation in 1962 and identifies Tehran as its location. It also lists Master of Business Administration and Executive MBA programs, alongside other management-related provision. However, the entry states that it was updated in March 2019. It is therefore useful as evidence of an established management-education institution, while current admissions, course availability, and qualification arrangements require fresh confirmation. A directory listing cannot demonstrate the effectiveness of a course or a graduate’s workplace performance. [s1](https://whed.net/institutions/IAU-016832)",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "knowledge-management",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Learning from organizational knowledge",
        "content": "A practical example comes from a Tehran course held in August 2017 by the Asian Productivity Organization, or APO, with the National Iranian Productivity Organization and Iran’s Ministry of Energy. It addressed knowledge management: the organized use and sharing of what people and institutions know. The curriculum connected this subject with innovation, information technology, productivity, and service quality. [s2](https://www.apo-tokyo.org/aponews/apo-conducts-training-for-km-in-the-public-sector/)\n\nThe organizer’s account describes case studies, comparative country material, a visit to Niroo Research Institute, and individual action plans for applying learning within participants’ organizations. These activities show a training design that moved beyond presentations toward observation and planned application. They do not establish whether those plans were subsequently implemented or produced lasting improvements. The account is a report of a particular international course hosted in Iran, rather than a representative survey of Iranian managers. [s2](https://www.apo-tokyo.org/aponews/apo-conducts-training-for-km-in-the-public-sector/)",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "management-in-training-institutions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Training institutions also need management capabilities",
        "content": "Management development also appears within vocational education itself. UNESCO’s terms of reference for an instructor-training modernization project in Karaj include strengthening the capabilities of managers, researchers, and master trainers. The document connects institutional development with a revised approach to delivering training. This is evidence that the project treats organizational capability as part of educational improvement; it is not an evaluation showing that management performance improved. [s3](https://iran.un.org/en/download/184181/294666)\n\nTogether, these examples distinguish subject-based management education, focused professional courses, and organizational development inside a training institution. Their purposes overlap, but their results require different evidence. A program listing establishes subject coverage; a course report establishes documented activities; evidence of workplace change requires subsequent observation. This distinction is especially useful for international readers encountering unfamiliar qualification names or institutional claims. The available material introduces the field, while leaving national participation, regional access, and independently assessed effectiveness unresolved.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
