    "description": "Entrepreneurship education in Iran includes university learning and initiatives to develop entrepreneurs and their mentors. Research on students provides evidence about intentions and perceived capabilities, which must be distinguished from verified business creation or survival.",
    "tags": ["Entrepreneurship", "University education", "Business skills", "Mentorship", "Startups", "Education research"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:49:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:49:52Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Entrepreneurship Education in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iranian entrepreneurship education, university research, mentor training, and the distinction between intentions and business outcomes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "learning-and-intention",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Learning about enterprise and deciding to pursue it",
        "content": "Entrepreneurship education involves learning associated with identifying opportunities and considering the creation or development of a venture. Iranian university research offers an important distinction: a student’s intention to become an entrepreneur is a different outcome from establishing an operating business. A study by Saeid Karimi and colleagues examined elective and compulsory entrepreneurship programs through questionnaires administered before and after participation. Its published abstract reports that elective programs increased entrepreneurial intention significantly, while the increase for compulsory programs was not statistically significant. [s1](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jsbm.12137)\n\nBoth forms of provision showed positive changes in perceived behavioral control and subjective norms. In this research framework, these concern a person’s perceived ability to act and the social expectations surrounding that action. The distinction between elective and compulsory learning is educationally significant, but the abstract does not establish that choosing a course caused better outcomes. Nor does it report a national business-formation rate attributable to education. [s1](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jsbm.12137)",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evaluating-programs",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Students and educators may assess teaching differently",
        "content": "Research on entrepreneurship programs in Iran’s applied-science and technology universities examined the perspectives of students and educators. Its abstract reports disagreement between the groups about relationships involving teaching methods and program impact. It also acknowledges that the selection of universities limits generalization. This supports a careful conclusion: evaluating provision requires attention to what learners experience, rather than relying exclusively on educators’ judgments. It does not justify labeling all Iranian entrepreneurship teaching effective or ineffective. [s2](https://www.emerald.com/et/article-abstract/61/7-8/781/72918/Evaluating-impact-of-entrepreneurship-education?redirectedFrom=fulltext)\n\nThe inspected material for these studies consists of published abstracts, so their detailed instruments, implementation conditions, and full statistical analyses are outside this account. Reported associations and changes should therefore remain attached to the specific studies. They should not be converted into claims about business survival, income, or jobs without follow-up evidence measuring those outcomes. [s1](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jsbm.12137) [s2](https://www.emerald.com/et/article-abstract/61/7-8/781/72918/Evaluating-impact-of-entrepreneurship-education?redirectedFrom=fulltext)",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mentor-development",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Developing the people who support entrepreneurs",
        "content": "Entrepreneurship learning also extends beyond student courses. UNIDO documents an Iranian information and communication technology initiative combining startup-ecosystem analysis, training for entrepreneurs and mentors, and a proposed virtual entrepreneurship hub. Its page identifies the Erasmus Centre for Entrepreneurship as the provider for a series of trainer-development programs. This introduces another educational layer: improving the capabilities of people who coach or support entrepreneurs. [s3](https://www.unido.org/training-startup-coaches-in-Iran)\n\nThe page says applications for that edition are closed. It should consequently be read as documentation of an initiative, rather than as a current enrollment invitation. Its stated competitiveness objectives also require separate outcome evidence. [s3](https://www.unido.org/training-startup-coaches-in-Iran)\n\nThe central distinction is between providing entrepreneurship learning, changing participants’ attitudes or perceived capabilities, and producing durable enterprises. The inspected sources illuminate the first stages more clearly than the last. A fuller national account needs current curricula, evidence beyond selected university settings, and follow-up research tracing actual ventures over time.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
